  "summary_so_far": "Hop 1: The tide arrives an hour early at the Grey Flats. Mara, measuring the water from her stilt-house, realizes her father's brass tide-clock is out of sync with the sea for the first time in three hundred years. In the rising surf, she spots a strange brass cylinder caught in the pilings.",
  "instructions_for_next_hop": "Increment 'hop' by 1. Replace 'text' with the next ~250-word installment of the story (do not just append, keep 'text' around 250 words total so the context doesn't explode, while updating 'summary_so_far' and 'story_bible' to retain memory across the 100 hops). Output ONLY valid JSON.",
  "text": "The tide came in an hour early. \n\nMara stood on the lower landing of the stilt-house, her boots already wet with grey foam that had no business being there until the third bell. Above her, inside the lantern-lit study, the great brass tide-clock ticked on with heavy, stupid confidence, its moon-dial still showing a mile of dry mud flat. For three hundred years, the clock and the sea had agreed to the second. Now the water was already lapping at the sixth iron rung of the ladder, warm as milk and smelling faintly of copper.\n\nShe leaned over the railing, the wind tangling her salt-stiff hair. Down in the weir-nets, where there should have been only slick kelp and flatfish, something new was wedged against the north pilings. The waves rolled over it, receded, and revealed a curve of riveted bronze, crusted not with barnacles, but with what looked like melted glass. \n\nIt was the size of a cistern. Every time the premature surf struck its flank, it gave off a dull, resonant hum that Mara felt in her teeth before she heard it. \n\nShe fetched the boat-hook and her oilskin coat. If the water kept rising at this speed, the lower storeroom would flood before midnight, and whatever was caught in the pilings would either tear the stilts down or sink back into the deep channel. As she climbed down the slick iron rungs, she realized the strangest part of all: the crabs were climbing up ahead of her, fleeing the sea."
}

