  "summary_so_far": "Hop 1: The tide arrives an hour early, warm and smelling of copper, out of sync with Mara's 300-year-old tide-clock. Crabs flee up the stilts as a glass-crusted bronze cylinder hums in the weir-nets.\nHop 2: Mara climbs down and chains the cylinder—an untethered, ancient diving bell—to the pilings. Through its fogged quartz viewport, she hears three rhythmic taps, spaced exactly one second apart.\nHop 3: A pale hand smears the condensation inside the viewport, pressing a notched brass wheel against the glass. Mara sees the outer hatch is soldered shut with lead. She hooks the bell to her salvage crane.\nHop 4: Mara winches the bell onto the deck, recognizing the brass gear as an escapement wheel matching her own tide-clock. Ignoring the stamped warning ('ARCHIVE ZERO - DO NOT UNSEAL'), she melts the lead seal with a blowtorch, causing the tide-clock upstairs to spin wildly out of control.\nHop 5: Mara pries open the hatch, releasing ozone-scented air. Inside is a young man in an archaic suit with brass calipers splinted to his fingers, cradling a lead-bound ledger. He gives her the blistering-hot escapement wheel, warns that 'The Registry dropped the third weight. The Flats aren't coming back up,' and collapses.\nHop 6: Mara drags the unconscious youth onto the deck and into her workshop. Beneath the floorboards, the copper-smelling water rises past the highest historical flood-notch. She discovers a faint mechanical ticking inside the boy's collarbone and examines the lead ledger, which is locked by a mechanism requiring the calipers strapped to his fingers.",
  "instructions_for_next_hop": "Increment 'hop' by 1. Replace 'text' with the next ~250-word installment of the story (keep 'text' around 250 words total, updating 'summary_so_far' and 'story_bible' to retain memory across the 100 hops). Output ONLY valid JSON.",
  "text": "Mara dropped the scorching gear into her canvas apron pocket and leaned into the hatch. Grabbing the boy by the leather harness under his arms, she hauled him out onto the wet planks. For all his soaked, heavy linen, he weighed shockingly little—like a bundle of hollow bird bones and dry reeds.\n\nBeneath the deck grating, the water slapped against the cross-braces. Mara froze, looking down between her boots. The tide had already swallowed the sixth iron bolt on the main piling—the highest flood-mark her grandfather had ever notched into the ironwood. And it was still rising, swirling with oily, copper-bright eddies in the lantern light.\n\nShe dragged the stranger across the threshold into her workshop and kicked the salt-swollen door shut. Setting him on the charting table among dried sponges and callipers, she checked his pulse. Beneath the hollow of his throat, right where his collarbone met his sternum, she felt not a heartbeat, but a crisp, rapid *tick-tick-tick*, like a pocket watch muffled in wool.\n\nHis fingers remained curled around the lead-bound ledger. Gently, she pried his right hand loose, though his left—the one splinted with the brass Vernier calipers—was hooked directly into a slotted bronze clasp on the book’s fore-edge. The calipers weren't medical splints; they were a key, their notched tips engaged with tumblers inside the lock.\n\nStamped into the soft lead cover were the same block letters as the bell, followed by a depth measurement: *ARCHIVE ZERO — 400 FATHOMS BELOW DATUM.*\n\nThere was no water that deep within five hundred miles of the Grey Flats."
}

