  "summary_so_far": "Hops 1-3: The tide arrives an hour early, warm and smelling of copper. Mara chains an ancient, humming bronze diving bell caught in her weir-nets to the pilings. Inside the fogged viewport, a pale hand presses a notched brass gear against the glass.\nHops 4-5: Mara winches the soldered bell onto her deck. Melting the lead seal ('ARCHIVE ZERO - DO NOT UNSEAL') causes her grandfather's tide-clock to break. Inside is a young man with brass calipers splinted to his fingers and a lead ledger. He hands her the hot escapement wheel, warns that 'The Registry dropped the third weight. The Flats aren't coming back up,' and collapses.\nHop 6: Mara drags the boy into her workshop as the tide passes the highest historical flood-mark. She hears a clockwork ticking inside his chest and discovers the calipers on his hand are a key fitted into the ledger's lock, stamped '400 FATHOMS BELOW DATUM.'\nHop 7: Mara manipulates the boy's splinted fingers to unlock the ledger. Its pages are thin copper foil etched with tidal equations and a cross-section map showing the Grey Flats as merely a thin crust over a four-hundred-fathom mechanical abyss. Water begins seeping up through the workshop floorboards, and the boy's chest-ticking begins to slow.",
  "instructions_for_next_hop": "Increment 'hop' by 1. Replace 'text' with the next ~250-word installment of the story (keep 'text' around 250 words total, updating 'summary_so_far' and 'story_bible' to retain memory across the 100 hops). Output ONLY valid JSON.",
  "text": "Mara’s fingers trembled as she took the boy’s cold wrist. Instead of pulling his hand away from the book, she pinched the thumb-wheel on his brass splint. As the Vernier scale slid backward by three millimeters, the bronze prongs inside the clasp shifted. A tiny spring clicked within the spine.\n\nThe heavy lead cover fell open, exhaling the dry, sharp scent of verdigris and old tallow.\n\nThe pages were not paper. They were sheets of beaten copper foil, thin as dried onion skin, each one densely etched with stylus-scratched diagrams and columns of tiny script. She turned the first leaf, its metallic edge chiming softly against the next. Across the double spread was a cross-section of the coast. She recognized the jagged tooth of the lighthouse twelve miles north, and the wide, flat shelf of the Grey Flats.\n\nExcept the shelf wasn't solid bedrock.\n\nIn the etching, the Flats were a perforated limestone ceiling—a mere vaulted roof suspended over a vertical chasm four hundred fathoms deep. Suspended inside that abyss were three colossal counterweights chained to cyclopean gears. Two were drawn at the bottom of the trench. The third had a red stylus slash through its chain.\n\n*Tick... tick...*\n\nMara looked up. The rapid rhythm inside the boy’s sternum was losing momentum, dragging like an unwound pendulum. At the exact same moment, a ribbon of warm, reddish water squeezed up through the tongue-and-groove floorboards, pooling around the legs of the charting table."
}

