  "text": "He sucked in a ragged breath that tasted of canned air and verdigris, his calloused right hand flying to his chest. When his fingers brushed the spinning teeth of the sixty-two-pin wheel, his sea-glass eyes widened in horror.\n\n\"You seated it,\" he rasped, his voice scraping like dry pumice. \"You shouldn't have seated it. I pulled it out so the Registry couldn't hear me ticking.\"\n\n\"You were dying,\" Mara said, steadying him by the shoulders as he tried to sit up. Through the wet wool of his sleeve, she could feel the rapid, mechanical tremor of the gear-train vibrating through his collarbone.\n\n\"I’m a regulator,\" he said, looking down at the crimson water now lapping over the tops of her boots. \"Kael. Third Sub-Basin. When they cut the third weight loose to drown the Flats, they tried to weld my arbor shut so the flood-valves couldn't be wound back. I climbed into the bell and pulled my own escapement to play dead.\"\n\nBeneath the floorboards, a deep, subterranean groan shuddered through the ironwood pilings. Bottles of iron-gall ink slid off Mara’s shelves, shattering silently into the rising red broth. Outside the salt-crusted window, the bronze diving bell tilted sharply as the deck planks canted five degrees to the west.\n\n\"The limestone shelf is settling into the trench,\" Kael said, gripping the edge of her workbench with his brass-splinted hand. His eyes caught the shattered pendulum of her grandfather’s tide-clock on the wall, and he froze. \"Where did you get a sixty-two-count arbor above datum?\"\n\n\"My grandfather,\" Mara said, snatching the heavy lead ledger from the desk before the water could reach it. \"Ask me after we're in the skiff.\""
}

