  "text": "\"Wait,\" Kael gasped. As Mara hauled his arm over her shoulder, he lunged toward the wall with his splinted hand, wrenching the shattered brass movement straight out of her grandfather’s oak clock case. He shoved the broken mainspring and the sixty-two-pin arbor into his oilskin pouch. \"If this was milled for the Third Sub-Basin, we need the alloy.\"\n\nOutside on the porch, the fog had turned the color of bruised plum, thick with the stench of sulfur and boiled kelp. The two-ton bronze diving bell suddenly broke loose from the winch drum, sliding down the canted planks and smashing through the railing. As it plunged into the crimson swell, the chain still looped around the northeast piling pulled taut. \n\nWith a sound like cannon fire, the ironwood piling sheared in half. The entire house lurched downward.\n\nMara threw the lead ledger into the bilge of her narrow, lateen-rigged skiff hanging from the davits, shoved Kael over the gunwale, and slashed the hemp falls with her rigging knife. The boat hit the hot, swirling water stern-first. Wavelets of rust-red brine slopped over the transom as she shipped the oars and pulled hard, backing them clear just as her workshop roof folded inward into a vortex of foam and floating parchment.\n\nIn the sudden quiet, the rhythmic *clack-clack-clack* of Kael’s sternum echoed against the cedar ribs of the hull, transferring straight through the planking into the sea.\n\n\"Water carries sound four times faster than air,\" Kael whispered, pressing a palm over his racing brass heart. \"The Registry's hydrophones on the trench wall just heard me wake up. Pull for the relief chimney—Spire Four.\""
}

