  "text": "Mara leaned into the ash sweeps, her palms blistering as she pulled northwest against a current thick with pumice and dead brittle-stars. Every stroke sent a shiver through the cedar hull, and between the splash of the blades, the skiff acted like a sounding board for Kael’s chest. *Clack-clack-clack.* Even through the soles of her boots, she could feel the sixty-two-pin wheel biting against his ribs.\n\n\"Get off the floorboards,\" she hissed, glancing back into the plum-colored murk where the roof peak of her home had just vanished beneath a greasy slick of air bubbles. \"Sit on the coiled painter. The wood is coupling you to the water.\"\n\nKael shifted onto the rope coil at the bow, his face gray with sweat. With his splinted left hand, he fished a curled, jagged shard of her grandfather’s broken mainspring from his pouch. \"Rope won't stop low-frequency resonance. Only phosphor-bronze dampens a regulator without shearing the teeth.\"\n\nBefore she could stop him, he wedged the spring steel beneath the oscillating pallet in his sternum. \n\nThe sharp *clack* died instantly, replaced by a muffled, grinding wheeze. Kael arched his back, biting his lip until it bled as his heartbeat stuttered against the friction brake. \n\nAhead, the fog parted around Spire Four—a hollow tower of fused worm-tubes and riveted iron plates rising forty feet above the swell, venting warm, metallic steam. \n\nAs the skiff’s stem scraped the barnacled landing iron, a high, crystalline *ping* rang through the water, vibrating the lead ledger in the bilge. Somewhere out in the mist, a Registry acoustic sweep had just bounced off the spire."
