  "text": "The second *ping* followed three seconds later, sharper, vibrating the fillings in Mara’s teeth. Out in the plum-colored fog, the low, rhythmic thrum of a Registry screw-propeller began to churn the swell.\n\n\"They’re triangulating on the hollow hull,\" Kael wheezed, clutching his sternum where a dark bloom of blood and clock-oil soaked through his waxed linen. Beneath the jammed spring-shard, the sixty-two-pin wheel strained, his pulse fluttering in his throat like a trapped moth.\n\nMara didn’t hesitate. She slung the heavy lead ledger over her shoulder by its strap, grabbed the skiff’s oak bilge-plug, and wrenched it free. Rust-red water geysered around her ankles. As the boat began to settle, she hauled Kael onto the slick, barnacle-crusted rungs of Spire Four.\n\nTen feet above the tide-line, a circular bronze hatch was recessed into the calcified worm-tubes. In its center sat not a wheel, but a Vernier keyway—two parallel slots notched with vernier increments.\n\n\"Your hand,\" Mara whispered. \n\nThe propeller wash grew louder; a pale yellow searchlight carved through the mist fifty yards out, turning the steaming water the color of bruised sulphur. \n\nKael raised his splinted left hand, his fingers trembling. Mara guided the brass calipers fused to his knuckles into the keyway, sliding his thumb until the vernier marks aligned at zero-four-zero. Deep inside the iron plate, counter-tumblers dropped with a greased *thud*.\n\nThey tumbled into the pitch-black, roaring warmth of the spire’s chimney and dogged the hatch shut just as the acoustic sweep struck the outer hull like a hammer blow."
