  "instructions_for_next_hop": "Increment 'hop' by 1. Replace 'text' with the next ~250-word installment of the story (keep 'text' around 250 words total, updating 'summary_so_far' and 'story_bible' to retain memory across the 100 hops). Output ONLY valid JSON.",
  "text": "The bronze rungs were slick with mineral grease and hot condensation. Mara climbed down into the updraft, the heavy lead ledger thumping against her spine with every step. Above her, Kael descended one-handed, his caliper-splinted fingers clattering rhythmically against the rails while his chest beat its metallic cadence: *clack-tock, clack-tock.*\n\nTen fathoms down, the riveted iron cylinder gave way to raw, chiseled stone. The transition was immediate. The hollow, ringing acoustics of the upper chimney vanished, swallowed by thirty feet of dense, fossil-packed limestone. The roar of the outer sea died away, replaced by the deep, barometric hum of pressurized air keeping the shaft dry.\n\n\"We're under the shelf,\" Kael called down, his voice suddenly flat and close. \"The stone will drink the sound now. Their hydrophones only hear the skin of the water.\"\n\nMara’s boots struck a grated iron landing. She stepped back, wiping warm salt-mist from her eyes. Pale green bioluminescent lichen grew thick along the mortar seams here, casting a corpse-light over the Sluice-Gallery.\n\nThe chamber widened around a circular moon pool of black, oil-still water, held back from rising by the spire's internal air pressure. Directly above the pool hung a colossal windlass, its gear-teeth sheared clean off. Dangling from the drum was a length of anchor chain with links as thick as Mara’s torso.\n\nShe walked to the edge of the grating and looked at the lowest link. It had not snapped from age or rust. The iron was bright and silver-scored, melted cleanly through at a forty-five-degree angle by an industrial thermal-lance.\n\n\"They didn't just drop it,\" Mara whispered, touching the cooled slag. \"They cut it free.\""
