  "text": "Kael dropped from the bottom rung, his boots ringing softly against the iron grating. He didn't look at the melted chain. Instead, his sea-glass eyes tracked a thinner line—a taut cable of braided phosphor-bronze that ran down through a greased guide-ring at the edge of the moon pool, vanishing into the black water.\n\n\"The Registry Auditors came down here before the tide turned,\" Kael said, his chest ticking steadily in the damp air. \"A thermal-lance leaves slag that smells like burnt garlic and ozone. You can still taste it.\"\n\nMara breathed through her mouth. Beneath the salt and wet limestone, the bitter, metallic tang was still there. \"Why cut the weight? If the Flats drown, the Registry loses the salt-pans too.\"\n\n\"They don't care about the salt anymore,\" Kael said, walking toward a narrow cylinder of riveted brass hanging from a davit beside the windlass. It was a sounder's drop-cage, barely wide enough for two people to stand chest-to-chest, fitted with thick quartz bulls-eyes and an internal hand-crank. \"The weight didn't just hold the ceiling vents shut. When it hit the floor at four hundred fathoms, it crushed the primary intake manifold. They're trying to flood Archive Zero.\"\n\nHe reached with his right hand to pull the drop-cage's hatch open, then froze. He pointed his caliper-splinted left fingers at the cable sleeve atop the capsule.\n\nThe heavy iron friction-shoe that regulated the cage's descent speed lay in two pieces on the catwalk, neatly severed by the same lance. \n\n\"If we release the davit latch,\" Mara said, peering into the cramped brass shell, \"we'll free-fall the whole way down.\""
