  "instructions_for_next_hop": "Increment 'hop' by 1. Replace 'text' with the next ~250-word installment of the story (keep 'text' around 250 words total, updating 'summary_so_far' and 'story_bible' to retain memory across the 100 hops). Output ONLY valid JSON.",
  "text": "\"Wait,\" Mara said. She reached into her coat and pulled out the jagged, coiled ribbon of tempered blue steel—the shattered mainspring from her grandfather’s tide-clock. \"The housing above the sleeve is slotted for a leaf-spring. If we pack the coil inside and jam the hand-crank against it, the steel will bite the bronze cable as we drop.\"\n\nKael examined the greasy slot atop the capsule, measuring the clearance with his caliper-fingers. \"Tempered steel against phosphor-bronze. It’ll shred the cable’s outer weave, and the friction will heat the roof of the cage like a stove-plate. Toward the bottom, the spring might snap altogether.\"\n\n\"Do you have another way down four hundred fathoms?\"\n\nHe looked at the black water lapping the lip of the moon pool, then took the steel coil. Working together, they wrestled the stiff, oily ribbon into the overhead sleeve, wedging the thicker arbor-end against the internal brake lever. \n\nThey squeezed inside. The brass cylinder was so narrow that Mara’s shoulder pressed against Kael’s sternum; through her damp wool coat, she felt the sharp, metallic *tock-tock* of his heart against her ribs. The smell of old tallow, verdigris, and his salt-cured linen filled the tiny space.\n\nKael dogged the hatch shut, plunging them into near-darkness save for the green glow filtering up through the lower quartz bulls-eye. He gripped the iron brake lever with his good hand; Mara braced her boots against the curvature of the wall and laid her hands over his.\n\n\"Hold on,\" Kael whispered, and kicked the davit release trip-rod.\n\nThe drop-cage plunged through the moon pool with a hollow boom, and the braided cable above their heads began to scream."
