  "text": "Water swallowed the capsule, turning the green twilight outside the lower quartz lens to pitch. The drop-cage bucked violently against the guide cable as they cleared the bottom of the limestone shelf and entered the open abyss.\n\nAbove their heads, the improvised brake shrieked in a high, grinding soprano. A shower of orange sparks rained down from the cable sleeve, hissing as they hit the damp condensation on their coats. Mara braced her knees against the curving brass wall, her muscles aching as she and Kael hauled down on the iron lever with all their weight. \n\n\"One hundred and fifty fathoms!\" Kael shouted over the din, reading the spinning brass dial bolted beside the hatch. The air inside the narrow cylinder was rapidly growing stifling, thick with the acrid stench of boiling tallow and scorched steel. The roof above them radiated a fierce, dry heat.\n\nThrough the soles of her boots, Mara felt the deep, slow thrum of the abyssal tide-engine far below—and answering it, the frantic, amplified *clack-clack* of the 62-pin gear in Kael’s chest. \n\nOutside the thick quartz lens, the blackness suddenly bloomed with pale, cold sparks of blue light.\n\nMara squinted through the smoke. They were not fish. Three spherical brass hulls, each trailing long, articulated copper feelers like the whiskers of a catfish, were gliding out of the dark water, converging on the phosphor-bronze cable.\n\n\"Listeners,\" Kael breathed, his sea-glass eyes wide in the red glow of the overheating sleeve. \"Registry acoustic mines. The cable’s screaming is drawing them in—and if we brake any harder to stop the noise, the spring will snap.\""
