  "text": "A high, crystalline singing vibrated through the hull, like a wet finger drawn around the rim of a crystal goblet. \n\n\"If you undog that hatch at three hundred and ninety fathoms,\" Mara said, her voice tight in the foul, oil-thick air, \"the sea will come in like a cannonball. We'll be paste before we swim a yard.\"\n\nKael froze, his caliper-splinted hand hovering over the iron dog-latch. His sea-glass eyes shifted down to the tilted floor-lens—now pressed nearly vertical against the dome’s sloping shoulder.\n\nThe heavy bronze rim of their cage had wedged itself into a V-shaped truss of the Weir's outer ribbing, forming a crude seal over a single, spiderwebbed pane of three-inch leaded glass. Behind that pane, the orange sodium light wasn't refracted through water. Dust motes drifted lazily in dry, compressed air. Ten feet inside the glass hung a catwalk of perforated iron grating.\n\n\"The crown of the vault trapped an air-head when the counterweight punched the center,\" Kael breathed. \"The internal pressure is holding the sea back at the fracture line.\"\n\n\"Not for long,\" Mara said, watching a thread of black water weep through the putty around the dome's glazing. \"And our floor-lens is three inches of solid quartz.\"\n\n\"It's seated from the inside for maintenance,\" Kael said. He pulled her grandfather’s steel clock-arbor from his belt, fitting its squared tang into the recessed lugs of the lens’s brass retaining ring. \"Brace your boots against the brake-housing. When I back this ring off, the air pressure inside the dome is going to blow the dome-pane and the quartz right back into our laps.\""
