  "instructions_for_next_hop": "Increment 'hop' by 1. Replace 'text' with the next ~250-word installment of the story (keep 'text' around 250 words total, updating 'summary_so_far' and 'story_bible' to retain memory across the 100 hops). Output ONLY valid JSON.",
  "text": "Breathing the fifty-atmosphere air was like inhaling warm tallow. Every intake scraped down Mara’s windpipe, heavy with the stink of scorched whale-oil from the flickering sodium lamps overhead. Above them, the broken diamond of the dome shrieked like a steam-whistle, venting their bubble into the crushing dark. With every second of escaped air, the mirror-flat boundary of the black ocean below rose another six inches toward the iron grating beneath their boots.\n\n\"Don't run!\" Kael’s voice came out as a high, reedy chime, absurdly bright against the bass roar of the escaping air. He gripped her elbow with his good hand, his brass-splinted fingers glinting under the amber lamps. \"The nitrogen will make you drunk, and the density will tear your lungs. Glide!\"\n\nThey moved along the swaying catwalk like waders in chest-deep surf, the thick air offering physical resistance against their oilskins. In Kael’s open tunic, the sixty-two-pin escapement wheel ticked with frantic, tiny hammer-blows, racing to match the acoustic pressure pulsing up from the flooded depths below.\n\nFifty yards ahead, the catwalk terminated against the central axis of the dome: a massive, barnacle-crusted cylinder of cast bronze that dropped straight through the rising water-table toward the sea floor. Set into its flank just above the catwalk was a circular hatch—the Index Lock.\n\nMara reached it first, her vision swimming with bright, narcotic sparks at the edges. She threw her weight against the outer spinning-wheel. It didn't budge. Beneath the wheel, three concentric brass rings were set into the doorplate, etched with microscopic vernier scales.\n\nBelow their boots, cold black water lapped through the iron grating, hissing as it touched the hot sodium-lamp condensers."
}

