ngs. The outer hatch unlatches, and they tumble inside just as the catwalk floods.",
  "instructions_for_next_hop": "Increment 'hop' by 1. Replace 'text' with the next ~250-word installment of the story (keep 'text' around 250 words total, updating 'summary_so_far' and 'story_bible' to retain memory across the 100 hops). Output ONLY valid JSON.",
  "text": "The freezing water bit through Mara’s greased boots, rising past her ankles to her calves in the span of three breaths. A weird, detached euphoria floated at the back of her skull—the nitrogen crowding her blood, making the shrieking dome overhead sound like a choir of glass flutes. She had to bite the inside of her cheek until she tasted copper to force her eyes to focus on the lead ledger.\n\n\"Give me the datum!\" Kael chirped, his high, compressed voice cracking. He jammed his left hand against the doorplate, fitting the jaws of his finger-splinted calipers into the notches of the outer brass ring. \n\nMara wrenched the lead covers apart, salt-crust flaking into the rising black water. \"Four hundred fathoms,\" she piped back, her tongue feeling thick as salted leather. \"Harmonic sixty-two. Offset... three-point-nine.\"\n\nKael twisted his wrist, bone and brass grating together as he spun the outer ring to four hundred, then pinched the middle dial to three-point-nine. The innermost ring had no notches—only a square keyhole at its center. With his right hand, Kael drew the tapered steel arbor of her grandfather’s tide-clock from his belt and drove it into the hole.\n\nIn his chest, the exposed escapement wheel gave a sudden, violent *clack*, aligning its magnetic teeth with the lock’s internal tumblers. Kael wrenched the arbor sixty-two ticks clockwise.\n\nA heavy, greased *thump* echoed through the bronze column. The locking dogs retracted. As the rising sea surged over their thighs, extinguishing the lowest sodium lamps in clouds of scalding steam, they threw their weight against the hatch, tumbled into the dry cylindrical lock, and slammed the iron door against the roaring flood."
