  "summary_so_far": "Hops 1-5: As a coppery tide floods the Grey Flats, Mara salvages an ancient bronze diving bell ('ARCHIVE ZERO'). Melting its seal shatters her grandfather's tide-clock mainspring. Inside is a boy with brass calipers splinted to his fingers and a lead ledger. He hands her a hot 62-pin escapement wheel, warns that 'The Registry dropped the third weight,' and collapses.\nHops 6-10: Mara uses the boy's calipers to open the ledger ('400 FATHOMS BELOW DATUM'), learning the Flats sit over an abyss. When the clockwork in his chest stops, she inserts the 62-pin gear into his sternum, reviving him. Kael warns his ticking chest is a beacon for the Registry. Taking her grandfather's clock arbor and broken mainspring, they flee as the bell drags her stilts-house under.\nHops 11-16: At Spire Four, Kael muffles his chest with a mainspring shard. They descend twenty fathoms to the Sluice-Gallery, finding the counterweight chain and drop-cage brake severed by a thermal-lance. Mara rigs her grandfather's mainspring as a friction-brake, and they drop into the moon pool.\nHops 17-20: Registry acoustic mines sever the upper cable at 150 fathoms. Kael slams the brake, welding the mainspring to the lower cable. Slowing past 310 fathoms, they see the Illuminated Weir at 390 fathoms—a dome crushed by the fallen Third Counterweight. Their cage slams onto the counterweight and tips against the dome.\nHops 21-24: Kael unscrews the cage's floor-lens to equalize the 50-atmosphere pressure. They scramble onto the catwalk as the dome's air bubble vents and water rises. Fighting nitrogen narcosis, Mara reads the ledger's coordinates while Kael uses his calipers and the clock arbor to unlock the central column's Index Lock. They tumble inside and seal the hatch against the flood.\nHop 25: Inside the quiet lock, they secure the inner wheel. Kael explains they cannot drop the pressure to surface normal without boiling Mara's blood, so he vents the lock slowly to the Archive's twelve-bar standard, filling the chamber with freezing fog as they begin descending the spiral stair inside the column.",
  "instructions_for_next_hop": "Increment 'hop' by 1. Replace 'text' with the next ~250-word installment of the story (keep 'text' around 250 words total, updating 'summary_so_far' and 'story_bible' to retain memory across the 100 hops). Output ONLY valid JSON.",
  "text": "The iron door seated with a dull, hydraulic thud, cutting off the shriek of the dying dome. Inside the bronze cylinder, the silence was sudden and heavy, broken only by the frantic, tinny *tick-tick-tick* of Kael’s sternum and the wet heave of Mara’s lungs.\n\nKael spun the inner dog-wheel, locking the sea out, then yanked the grandfather clock’s steel arbor from the interior keyhole. Outside, fifty atmospheres of black water hammered against the convex hull, making the rivets groan.\n\nMara slid down the curved wall, giggling softly at the trails of violet sparks drifting across her vision. Her fingers felt miles away from her wrists. \"We're inside,\" she squeaked, her voice still cartoonishly high in the dense air. \"Why is the floor tilting?\"\n\n\"Nitrogen,\" Kael squeaked back, kneeling beside her. He didn't laugh; his sea-glass eyes were sharp, his blood pumped by brass and oil rather than gas-drunk tissue. He reached up and pulled a heavy copper lever marked *SCRUB & BLEED—12 BAR*. \n\nImmediately, racks of glass cylinders along the walls began to bubble with pale green lime-water. A valve hissed overhead, venting the syrup-thick air. \n\n\"I can't drop us to surface pressure,\" Kael said, his voice deepening half an octave as the chamber bled down toward the Archive’s twelve-bar standard. \"You've breathed fifty atmospheres for ten minutes. If we decompress too fast, the gas will boil out of your marrow. We go down to the stacks at twelve atmospheres, through the heliox bath.\"\n\nAs the pressure dropped, the air chilled instantly, condensing into a blinding, freezing white fog that smelled of chalk and old whale oil. Through the mist, the metal grating beneath their boots vibrated—not from the sea, but with three deliberate, rhythmic clangs ascending from the dark spiral stair below."
}

