  "text": "*Clang. Clang-clang. Clang.*\n\nThe vibrations traveled up the central iron newel post and straight into the soles of Mara’s boots. The violet sparks in her eyes were fading as the thin, icy helium-oxygen mix hissed from the wall vents, purging the heavy nitrogen from her lungs, though a dull, needle-sharp ache had begun to bloom in her shoulders and knees.\n\nKael froze, pressing his brass-splinted fingers against the frost-rimed iron of the spiral stair. His chest ticked sharply in the cold fog, the exposed escapement wheel glinting behind his linen shirt like a trapped beetle.\n\n\"Is that the Registry?\" Mara whispered. Her teeth chattered violently; adiabatic cooling had turned their sweat to rime.\n\n\"No. Registry cutters don't knock,\" Kael said, his voice still reedy from the helium, though lower now as the pressure gauge on the bulkhead crept down past twenty atmospheres toward twelve. \"That’s Old Basin hammer-code. Someone is at the bottom of the stair. Wait—\"\n\n*Clang-clang-clang. Clang.*\n\nKael’s sea-glass eyes widened. \"'Manifold breached. Seal the staging lock.'\" He grabbed Mara’s bruised wrist, pulling her to her feet. \"Move. If the lower bulkhead gives before we lock the staging hatch at four hundred fathoms, this whole column becomes a chimney.\"\n\nThey plunged down the tight iron corkscrew into the white blindness. Mara clutched the lead ledger to her ribs with one arm, sliding her other gloved hand along the freezing rail. Step after perforated step clattered beneath them, spiraling past glowing green tubes of bubbling lime-water.\n\nTen fathoms down, the fog thinned into a layer of stagnant, oily bilge-mist. Below them, a circular bronze deck appeared—and beneath that deck, the iron floor suddenly bulged upward with a deafening, sub-audible boom."
