  "text": "The shockwave threw Mara off the last three steps. She hit the bronze deckplates on her bruised knees, the lead ledger skittering across the wet metal. Around the center of the floor, a heavy circular hatch was bowing upward like a blister, its iron collar groaning against twelve locking dogs—three of which had sheared clean off, their headless bolts ricocheting through the mist.\n\nA thin, lethal ribbon of fifty-atmosphere seawater hissed from the warped seam, slicing a groove into the column’s lime-water tubing.\n\n\"Vesper!\" Kael dropped beside the hatch, pressing his face toward the thick quartz bullseye set into its center.\n\nMara scrambled over, biting back the decompression ache in her shoulders. Through the salt-clouded quartz, illuminated by the dying amber glow of a flooding sub-chamber, she saw a gaunt figure in a battered copper diving helm and heavy oilskins. Water was already churning around his chest. In his gloved hand, he held a heavy brass wrench, tapping it one last time against the underside of the iron.\n\n*Dog the north wedge,* the wrench rapped.\n\n\"The pin is sheared!\" Kael shouted through the glass, heaving against the north dogging-lever. The iron cam slid home, but without a retaining pin, the hydraulic pressure immediately began forcing it back out.\n\nBelow the glass, Vesper pointed a stiff, gauntleted finger straight at Mara’s belt.\n\nWithout thinking, Mara yanked her grandfather’s tapered steel clock arbor from her sash. As Kael threw his full weight against the lever, chest-gears whining at a frantic pitch, Mara jammed the six-inch octagonal steel spindle into the sheared pinhole. \n\nThe fifty-bar water hammer slammed upward again. The steel arbor bit deep into the bronze housing, bending slightly, and held. Beneath the glass, the dark water closed over the copper helm."
