  "text": "The thin jet of seawater died to a salty weep. Mara stayed on her knees, staring at her grandfather’s octagonal steel arbor. It was bent ten degrees off-true, groaning under the weight of four hundred fathoms of ocean, never to be pulled free.\n\nBeneath the thick quartz bullseye, the amber light flickered against the brass faceplate of Vesper’s helmet. He wasn't dead—air bubbles still trailed from his helm's exhaust valve, fed by a heavy rubber umbilical snaking into the dark—but the manifold chamber was completely submerged. Slowly, the old keeper raised his wrench and tapped a tight, deliberate cadence against the underside of the glass before the current dragged him backward into the murk.\n\n\"What did he say?\" Mara asked. She flinched at the sound of her own voice; the twelve-bar helium mixture turned her ragged whisper into a high, reedy chime, like a glass harmonica.\n\nKael rested his calloused palm over the quartz, hissplinted brass calipers clicking against the iron bezel. \"*Rack Nine, Spindle Four,*\" he translated, his voice equally thin and birdlike in the dense gas. \"*Pull the Registry's warrant.*\"\n\nHe rose, hooking an arm under Mara’s bruised shoulder to haul her up. Needles of nitrogen pain pricked behind her kneecaps as she stood, clutching the lead ledger to her ribs.\n\n\"Can he survive down there?\" she breathed.\n\n\"As long as the main air-compressors in the nave keep turning,\" Kael said, turning toward the massive horizontal bulkhead set into the column wall. He fitted his left hand’s calipers into a recessed keyway and threw the counter-lever. The heavy bronze door swung inward with a sigh of dry, oil-scented warmth, revealing a cavern of towering brass card-looms stretching into cavernous twilight."
