  "text": "The air inside the Nave tasted of warm whale-oil and ozone. Above them, the curved ribs of the outer hull groaned under the resting bulk of the fallen counterweight, weeping condensation down onto rows of thirty-foot brass Jacquard looms. Instead of thread, the frames were strung with braided phosphor-bronze wire, suspending thousands of perforated metal plates that shivered in the draft of the ventilation turbines.\n\nMara’s left knee buckled as she stepped over the coaming. The nitrogen bubbles in her joints felt like ground glass. Kael caught her elbow, guiding her onto a grating bench beside an apothecary locker bolted to the bulkhead. He snapped open a green glass ampoule wrapped in wire mesh and held it under her nose.\n\n\"Breathe deep,\" his voice piped in the thin, Flute-like register of the heliox. \"Rich oxygen and camphor. It won't clear the bends, but it’ll shrink the pearls in your blood.\"\n\nShe inhaled the sharp, medicinal vapor until the spots faded from her vision. While she steadied herself, Kael reached into his waxed-linen shirt and pulled the shard of her grandfather’s mainspring from his sternum. Instantly, the sixty-two-pin escapement wheel in his chest rang out—a crisp, metallic *tick-tack* that echoed off the vaulted ceiling, locking into exact phase with the thrum of the overhead drive-shafts.\n\n\"You aren't muffling it anymore?\" she asked, her voice squeaking like a rubbed goblet.\n\n\"No need. Down here, I sound like the room,\" Kael said, tilting his chin toward the gloomy aisle markers. Then his sea-glass eyes narrowed. \n\nFar down the catwalk, in the shadows of Aisle Nine, a single loom was clattering violently—its heavy steel shear-blades dropping in rapid succession, chewing perforated bronze records into confetti."
