  "summary_so_far": "Hops 1-5: As a coppery tide floods the Grey Flats, Mara salvages an ancient bronze diving bell ('ARCHIVE ZERO'). Melting its seal shatters her grandfather's tide-clock mainspring. Inside is a boy with brass calipers splinted to his fingers and a lead ledger. He hands her a hot 62-pin escapement wheel, warns that 'The Registry dropped the third weight,' and collapses.\nHops 6-10: Mara uses the boy's calipers to open the ledger ('400 FATHOMS BELOW DATUM'), learning the Flats sit over an abyss. When the clockwork in his chest stops, she inserts the 62-pin gear into his sternum, reviving him. Kael warns his ticking chest is a beacon for the Registry. Taking her grandfather's clock arbor and broken mainspring, they flee as the bell drags her stilts-house under.\nHops 11-16: At Spire Four, Kael muffles his chest with a mainspring shard. They descend twenty fathoms to the Sluice-Gallery, finding the counterweight chain and drop-cage brake severed by a thermal-lance. Mara rigs her grandfather's mainspring as a friction-brake, and they drop into the moon pool.\nHops 17-20: Registry acoustic mines sever the upper cable at 150 fathoms. Kael slams the brake, welding the mainspring to the lower cable. Slowing past 310 fathoms, they see the Illuminated Weir at 390 fathoms—a dome crushed by the fallen Third Counterweight. Their cage slams onto the counterweight and tips against the dome.\nHops 21-25: Kael unscrews the cage's floor-lens to equalize the 50-atmosphere pressure. Fighting nitrogen narcosis, Mara reads the ledger's coordinates while Kael unlocks the central column's Index Lock. Inside, Kael vents the chamber toward the Archive's twelve-bar heliox standard, filling the cylinder with freezing fog as three rhythmic clangs ring up the spiral stair.\nHops 26-30: Kael translates the clangs as 'Manifold breached. Seal the staging lock.' On the 400-fathom deck, they find the old keeper Vesper trapped beneath the viewport. Mara jams her grandfather's steel clock arbor into a sheared pinhole to hold the hatch against the 50-bar flood. Submerged in his copper helm, Vesper taps 'Rack Nine, Spindle Four' before sinking away. Entering the Nave, Kael gives Mara a camphor-oxygen ampoule for the bends and un-muffles his chest. Racing down Aisle Nine, they find a pneumatic Red-Line telegraph remotely driving Spindle Four's guillotine, and Kael jams his own caliper-splinted hand into the feeder cam to save the final three plates.",
  "instructions_for_next_hop": "Increment 'hop' by 1. Replace 'text' with the next ~250-word installment of the story (keep 'text' around 250 words total, updating 'summary_so_far' and 'story_bible' to retain memory across the 100 hops). Output ONLY valid JSON.",
  "text": "Curled shavings of phosphor-bronze snowed into the catch-trough below Rack Nine. Mara limped down the iron catwalk behind Kael, the lead ledger heavy against her hip, her knee throbbing with every clang of the guillotine blade.\n\nNo figure stood at the console. Instead, a thick gutta-percha tube—painted the vermilion of the High Registry—snaked down from the buckled ceiling ribs and coupled directly into the loom’s pneumatic manifold. Every three seconds, the tube pulsed with a hiss of surface-compressed air, tripping the heavy iron pawl that fed Spindle Four into the shears.\n\n\"A Red-Line override,\" Kael piped, his reedy voice tight over the clatter. \"When the third counterweight failed to breach the inner vault, the surface clerks opened the purge valves from above.\"\n\nAbove the guillotine, the brass carousel of Spindle Four turned with relentless precision. Twelve plates had already been reduced to glittering scrap; only three remained on the braided wire loop, jerking downward toward the jaws.\n\nMara lunged for the manual clutch lever, but a heavy lead seal had been crimped over the slot. \"It’s locked out!\"\n\nThe pneumatic tube hissed. The third-to-last plate dropped into the throat. \n\nKael didn't hesitate. Before the cam could trip the falling blade, he thrust his left hand straight into the brass feeder-gears. The heavy steel teeth bit down on the Vernier calipers splinted to his fingers. Brass screeched against steel; sparks showered over the oil-wet floor, and the overhead drive-belt began to smoke and squeal on its pulley.\n\nKael’s chest mechanism skipped a beat—a harsh, metallic *clack*—as he braced his shoulder against the trembling frame. \"The wire, Mara! Cut the spindle loop before the belt snaps my wrist!\""
}

