ared pinhole to hold the hatch against the flood. Submerged in his helm, Vesper taps 'Rack Nine, Spindle Four.' In the Nave, Kael gives Mara a camphor-oxygen ampoule for the bends. At Rack Nine, they jam the pneumatic guillotine with Kael's splinted hand and the last mainspring shard, saving three plates stamped 'THE DRY LEASE.' A pneumatic canister drops from the surface; inside is a ticking hydrostatic fuse and a vial of quicksilver meant to dissolve the brass looms.",
  "instructions_for_next_hop": "Increment 'hop' by 1. Replace 'text' with the next ~250-word installment of the story (keep 'text' around 250 words total, updating 'summary_so_far' and 'story_bible' to retain memory across the 100 hops). Output ONLY valid JSON.",
  "text": "Frost bloomed instantly across the dispatch receiver’s bronze hatch, driven by the chill of four hundred fathoms of seawater pressing against the outer tube. \n\n\"Don't touch the latch,\" Kael chirped, his voice thin and reedy in the twelve-bar heliox. He stepped back, tucking his mangled left hand into the breast of his waxed-linen coat while his right drew a heavy spanner from the loom's tool-tray. \"If they sent a courier-shell down the Red-Line after the shear stalled, it won't be paper.\"\n\nMara tucked the three salvaged phosphor-bronze plates beneath her arm. In the green glow of the bioluminescent oil-lamp, she caught the human-readable header stamped along the top margin of the first plate before the punch-grid began: *INDENTURE OF THE FIRST BASIN — THE DRY LEASE.*\n\nBefore she could read the perforations, the dispatch cylinder hissed. A clockwork escapement inside the canister was winding down, much faster than the steady, heavy *clack-clack* of Kael’s sternum.\n\nKael hooked the spanner over the receiver's exhaust valve and wrenched it sideways, venting the compressed surface air away from them. Then he popped the hatch. \n\nThe leather-padded brass shell slid out onto the grating. Its nose-cone was unthreaded, revealing a thick glass ampoule filled with a heavy, shifting silver liquid—amalgamating quicksilver—strapped to a hydrostatic bellows fuse. \n\n\"Alchemist's mercury,\" Kael breathed, his sea-glass eyes widening. \"If that shatters on the grating, it’ll eat through the brass looms and the bronze floor-seals like sugar in hot tea.\"\n\nInside the glass, a tiny steel striker was already creeping toward the percussion cap, driven by the twelve-bar pressure of the Nave."
