  "instructions_for_next_hop": "Increment 'hop' by 1. Replace 'text' with the next ~250-word installment of the story (keep 'text' around 250 words total, updating 'summary_so_far' and 'story_bible' to retain memory across the 100 hops). Output ONLY valid JSON.",
  "text": "The corrugated brass bellows of the fuse groaned, collapsing inward under the twelve-bar weight of the Nave’s atmosphere. Another millimeter and the steel needle would kiss the fulminate cap.\n\n\"Lead doesn't amalgamate!\" Mara squeaked, the helium turning her panic into a birdlike trill. Dropping the three bronze plates onto the tool-tray, she unstrapped the heavy lead ledger from her belt. \"The covers—they're soft plumbum!\"\n\nUnderstanding flashed in Kael’s sea-glass eyes. Before the bellows could finish its creep, Mara yanked the thick lead plates of *400 FATHOMS BELOW DATUM* apart at the binding and wrapped the pliable metal around the glass vial like wet dough, crimping the edges over the creeping striker pin to choke its travel. \n\n\"Back in the breech!\" Kael ordered. \n\nShe shoved the lead-swaddled bomb nose-first back into the brass dispatch tube and slammed the frosted hatch. Kael threw his weight against the spanner, wrenching the manifold's three-way cock from *VENT* to *RETURN*. \n\nTwelve atmospheres of dense heliox roared into the breech behind the shell. With a hollow, pneumatic *thump* that shook salt-scale from the overhead line-shafts, the canister shot upward into the dark, riding a four-hundred-fathom pressure differential straight back toward the surface Registry's receiving room.\n\nFor ten seconds, they listened to the brass pipe sing as the shell climbed the abyss.\n\n\"They won't send a second,\" Kael whispered, his chest-escapement ticking hard against his ribs. He picked up the three perforated plates from the tray and nodded toward an idle Jacquard reader-loom at the center of the Nave. \"Come on. Let's hear what the tide-engine has to say.\""
}

