  "summary_so_far": "Hops 1-10: As a coppery tide floods the Grey Flats, Mara salvages a bronze diving bell ('ARCHIVE ZERO') containing Kael, a boy with brass calipers splinted to his fingers, a clockwork chest, and a lead ledger ('400 FATHOMS BELOW DATUM'). She inserts a 62-pin gear into his sternum to revive him, and they flee as the bell drags her stilts-house under.\nHops 11-20: At Spire Four, they descend to the Sluice-Gallery and take a sabotaged drop-cage into the moon pool, using Mara's grandfather's clock mainspring as a friction-brake. Acoustic mines sever the upper cable at 150 fathoms, and the cage crashes at 390 fathoms onto the fallen Third Counterweight, which has buckled the Illuminated Weir's dome.\nHops 21-28: Equalizing to 50 atmospheres and unlocking the Index Lock, they enter the central column and vent it to twelve-bar heliox. On the 400-fathom deck, they find Vesper trapped in the flooded manifold room below. Mara jams her grandfather's clock arbor into the hatch pinhole to hold back the sea. Submerged in his diving helm, Vesper taps 'Rack Nine, Spindle Four.'\nHops 29-34: In the Nave, Kael gives Mara a camphor-oxygen ampoule for the bends. At Rack Nine, they jam a pneumatic guillotine with the last mainspring shard, saving three phosphor-bronze plates ('INDENTURE OF THE FIRST BASIN — THE DRY LEASE'). When a mercury bomb drops down the Red-Line tube, they wrap it in Mara's lead ledger and fire it back up the shaft. As the bomb detonates far above, they feed the three plates into the central Translating Loom, which types out the truth: the Grey Flats are an artificial polder kept dry by the Tide-Engine under a 300-year lease that just expired.",
  "instructions_for_next_hop": "Increment 'hop' by 1. Replace 'text' with the next ~250-word installment of the story (keep 'text' around 250 words total, updating 'summary_so_far' and 'story_bible' to retain memory across the 100 hops). Output ONLY valid JSON.",
  "text": "High above, at the top of the four-hundred-fathom column, the returned shell found its mark. A dull, metallic *bong* traveled down the brass dispatch pipe, vibrating through the grated floor into Mara’s aching knees. A moment later, a trickle of warm, amalgamated sludge—lead, brass, and mercury—wept from the Red-Line’s gasket and hissed onto the deck.\n\n\"Scratch one receiving room,\" Kael squeaked softly.\n\nAt the center of the Nave stood the Translating Loom, a tall pulpit of blackened iron where an array of spring-loaded steel feeler-pins waited above a continuous roll of vellum. Mara slotted the three salvaged plates of *THE DRY LEASE* onto the brass carrier-chain. When Kael pulled the clutch lever, the overhead line-shaft caught with a greasy clatter.\n\nThe feeler-pins dropped. Where they met solid phosphor-bronze, they stayed up; where they found perforations, they plunged through, tripping mechanical type-bars that struck the vellum in rapid, machine-gun bursts of purple aniline ink.\n\nMara leaned over the platen, her eyes widening as the lines marched across the skin:\n\n*CLAUSE VII: THAT THE UPPER POLDER KNOWN AS THE GREY FLATS IS NOT LAND, BUT A BORROWED DISPLACEMENT PUMPED DRY BY THE ABYSSAL ENGINE. UPON EXPIRATION OF THE THREE-HUNDREDTH SOLAR YEAR, SHOULD THE SURFACE REGISTRY DEFAULT UPON THE BALLAST-TITHE OF SIX HUNDRED SOULS TO WIND THE MAIN WEIGHTS, THE SEA-COCKS SHALL REVERT TO OPEN.*\n\n\"It isn't a flood,\" Mara whispered, her reedy helium voice trembling as the second plate clicked under the needles. \"The ocean isn't rising. The floor is just... going back where it belongs.\""
}

