  "text": "The carrier-chain clanked, drawing the second and third plates beneath the steel feeler-pins. Purple ink spattered like crushed beetles across the vellum as the type-bars hammered out the codicil.\n\n*CLAUSE VIII: FAILING THE SIX HUNDRED, THE WEIR-PUMPS MAY BE RE-ENGAGED FOR ONE HUNDRED SOLAR YEARS ONLY BY DIRECT COUPLING OF THE GOVERNOR-ARBOR AT FIVE HUNDRED FATHOMS. THE REGULATOR MUST BEAR THE SIXTY-TWO-PIN DATUM ESCAPEMENT WITHIN LIVING BONE, TO TAKE UP THE SLACK OF THE PENDULUM IN PERPETUITY.*\n\nThe loom’s clutch threw itself out with a sharp *ping*. In the sudden quiet, the ticking inside Kael’s breastbone sounded terribly loud, echoing against the brass ribs of the vault.\n\nMara looked from the wet purple letters to the open placket of his waxed-linen shirt. Beneath the scarred skin of his sternum, the sixty-two-pin wheel—the gear she had pried from her grandfather’s ruined tall-case clock—turned with a steady, oil-slicked gleam.\n\n\"Your grandfather didn't salvage that clock from a wreck,\" Kael said, his high, helium-thinned voice flat. He touched the caliper-splints on his bruised fingers. \"He was an apprentice here sixty years ago. He stole the Datum wheel so the Registry couldn't force a regulator into the Sump when the lease came due. Vesper sent me up in the bell to find his house before midnight.\"\n\nOverhead, the main line-shaft groaned. The rhythmic *chug-hiss* of the air compressors faltered as the fallen counterweight ten fathoms above ground against the primary drive-chain. On the wall gauge, the pressure needle for Vesper’s umbilical began to dip."
