  "text": "A belt slapped violently against an overhead pulley, shedding a snow of dry leather flakes through the greenish lamp-glow. On the bulkhead, the brass needle of the manifold gauge trembled down from twelve bar toward eleven. Down in the black water beneath the vestibule deck, Vesper would already feel his copper helmet growing heavy against his collarbones, the air turning sour and thick.\n\n\"If the main shaft seizes, the check-valves on his hose will shut,\" Kael said. He reached into the loom's bed, unlatched the three heavy phosphor-bronze plates of the Dry Lease, and slung them onto a leather strap across his shoulder. Their jagged perforations clinked like armor. \"The Sump is another hundred fathoms down. Once I lock into the Zero-Spindle, the tide-engine will take the load off the upper chain and draw the counterweight clear.\"\n\n\"And leave you pinned to an axle in the dark for a hundred years,\" Mara said, her voice reedy and thin in the helium mix, though her throat ached with the thickness of the pressure. \"My grandfather didn't steal that wheel just to drown the Flats sixty years later. He kept the mainspring and the winding-arbor alongside it for a reason.\"\n\n\"A clock spring can't turn ten thousand tons of pig-iron and seawater, Mara.\"\n\n\"No,\" she said, wiping purple ink from her knuckles onto her wool skirt. \"But a clockmaker knows how to build a dead-beat escapement that doesn't chew up its own pallet-stones. Where is the hatch?\"\n\nKael hesitated, the sixty-two pins in his chest clicking in rapid, shallow time. Then he turned toward the apse of the vault, where a circular well of cast iron sat ringed by weeping tallow candles."
