  "text": "The tallow candles burned with squat, furiously bright blue flames in the compressed heliox, smelling of rendered mutton fat and wet iron. There was no hatch cover over the Sump-Well—only a lip of cast iron grooved deep by centuries of hemp and wire rope, dropping straight down through the floor of the rock like a gun barrel.\n\nWarm, greasy air breathed up from the shaft, carrying a slow, rhythmic thud that Mara felt in her molars before she heard it: the Abyssal Tide-Engine below, laboring against the dead weight of the ocean.\n\nSuspended over the drop hung a bosun's chair—little more than an oak plank threaded onto a braided phosphor-bronze cable, linked to a hand-cranked friction brake. Kael stepped onto the plank, hooking his right elbow around the cable to keep his splinted left hand clear. The three bronze plates slung across his back clanged against the iron rim.\n\n\"Get on behind me,\" he said, his voice high and nasal, like a reed pipe played in an empty cistern. \"Keep your skirts clear of the guide-sheaves. There are no safety dogs in this tube.\"\n\nMara clambered onto the narrow board, pressing her chest against the stiff, salt-crusted linen of his back and the cold edges of the Lease plates. Beneath his ribs, through flesh and brass, she felt the sixty-two pins of her grandfather's wheel beating against her sternum like a trapped bird.\n\nKael reached out with his boot and kicked the brake-pawl free. The drum whined, spitting sparks of tallow-grease, and the green glow of the Nave vanished upward as the dark swallowed them."
