  "text": "Mara’s fingers shook as she undid the salt-stiffened toggles of Kael’s waxed-linen smock. Beneath the cloth, the brass casing grafted to his sternum gleamed wetly with condensation and trace amounts of red lymph. Inside the bevelled recess, her grandfather’s 62-pin Datum gear whirred against two jewel-pivoted pallets, its rhythm tied to the heave of Kael’s lungs.\n\nWith his good right hand and the brass calipers splinted to his left, Kael slotted the three phosphor-bronze plates of the Dry Lease into the drum above the Zero-Spindle. Heavy steel reading-needles dropped into the perforations with a resonant *chunk-chunk-chunk*, translating the three-hundred-year covenant into mechanical throw. \n\n\"Once my sternum locks onto the mandrel, the torque will trip the clutch and haul the Third Counterweight off the chain,\" Kael said, his high, helium-sheared voice steady despite the sweat beading on his collarbone. \"The compressors will turn. Vesper will breathe. And the sea-doors will pump the Flats dry.\"\n\nHe braced his palms against the oiled bronze struts of the altar and leaned his chest toward the spinning three-pronged spindle.\n\n\"Wait,\" Mara cried, grabbing his belt. She stared past the mandrel, aligning the colossal bronze scaffolding with the memory of her sunken house. The Zero-Spindle stood precisely where the pantry dumbwaiter had been. \"In my house, when the dumbwaiter rope frayed, the box didn't fall. Grandfather built a double-weighted grasshopper catch under the floorboards—a maintaining-power detent so you could unhook the main arbor without stopping the train.\"\n\nShe looked down through the iron grating at the shivering mirror of mercury six feet below. Submerged just beneath the silver skin, bolted to the pontoon flange, was a curved lignum-vitae lever."
