  "text": "Kael held out his ruined left hand. The brass Vernier calipers were lashed to his index and middle fingers with salt-stiffened catgut, the metal stained dark with old blood. Instead of untying the splints, Mara took his whole wrist, guiding the sharp inside-jaws of the calipers directly into the open cavity of his sternum.\n\nBeneath his collarbones, the 62-pin Datum gear whirred in a blur of oiled teeth, smelling of hot tallow and copper. Mara squinted through the dense heliox fog, timing the rhythm. \"Breathe out and hold,\" she squeaked.\n\nKael exhaled. Mara drove the caliper points between the third and fourth arbors, twisting his wrist until the steel tip caught a tiny, recessed pawl deep behind his breastbone. A microscopic *snick* echoed against his ribs. A hidden spiral spring took the tension of his blood-pump, keeping the ruby pallets oscillating in empty air.\n\nWith her left hand, still numb and beading with silver droplets of mercury, she pinched the central boss of the 62-pin gear and drew it off his spindle.\n\nKael gasped, his knees buckling as his pulse dropped into a shallow, fluttering stutter. \"Thirty seconds on my auxiliary coil,\" he whispered, his sea-glass eyes dilating wide. \"It's slipping, Mara.\"\n\nShe turned to the monumental Zero-Spindle. The grasshopper levers beneath the grating were already groaning toward the end of their ninety-second arc, shedding quicksilver like sweat. Mara aligned the keyway of her grandfather’s stolen gear with the great bronze mandrel—only to find the shaft scored by a burr of sheared steel from the straining drive-chain."
