  "text": "The brass collar refused to slide past the jagged curl of steel. Below the grating, the lignum-vitae grasshopper arm clicked past its penultimate tooth; five seconds remained before the full weight of the drowned polder crashed back onto the uncoupled shaft.\n\nKael slumped against the bronze pillar, his lips turning the dull violet of bruised plum. Inside his chest, the tiny spiral spring whined as it ran down, the ruby pallets stuttering to a halt.\n\nMara didn’t look for a hammer. She snatched the heavy lead-bound ledger from Kael’s belt and brought its spine down hard against the face of the 62-pin gear. Once, twice—soft lead deforming against hardened clock-brass, shaving the steel burr off the mandrel in a bright spark that smelled of flint. The gear seated with a resonant *thrum*.\n\nNo time to fit the jumper-pinion. She grabbed Kael by the waxed-linen lapels and hauled his limp weight forward, pressing his open sternum directly against the rotating rim of the Datum gear. \n\n\"Forgive me,\" she squeaked into the fog.\n\nThe grasshopper spring let go. The massive Zero-Spindle took the load, its sixty-two teeth biting simultaneously into the phosphor-bronze notches of the Dry Lease above and the exposed contrate wheel inside Kael’s ribs. \n\nA deep, sub-audible bass note vibrated through the mercury pool, throwing up concentric rings of silver mist. Kael arched back with a sharp, ragged inhale as the Tide-Engine’s own mainspring began to beat his heart—sixty monumental ticks a minute. High overhead, the jammed drive-chain gave a deafening crack and began to climb."
