  "summary_so_far": "Hops 1-10: As a coppery tide floods the Grey Flats, Mara salvages a bronze diving bell ('ARCHIVE ZERO') containing Kael, a boy with brass calipers splinted to his fingers, a clockwork chest, and a lead ledger. She inserts a 62-pin gear from her grandfather's clock into his sternum to revive him, and they flee as her stilts-house sinks.\nHops 11-20: At Spire Four, they take a sabotaged drop-cage down the moon pool, using her clock's mainspring as a brake. Mines sever the cable at 150 fathoms, and they crash at 390 fathoms onto the fallen Third Counterweight.\nHops 21-28: Equalizing to 50 atmospheres and unlocking the Index Lock, they vent the central column to twelve-bar heliox. At 400 fathoms, they find Vesper trapped in the flooded manifold room below. Mara jams her clock arbor into the hatch pinhole to hold the seal. Vesper taps 'Rack Nine, Spindle Four.'\nHops 29-39: In the Nave, they save three phosphor-bronze plates ('THE DRY LEASE') and send a mercury bomb up the pneumatic tube to destroy the upper receiving room. The Translating Loom reveals the Grey Flats are an artificial polder whose 300-year lease expired when the Registry refused a 600-life 'ballast-tithe.' Only the 62-pin Datum gear—stolen sixty years ago by Mara's grandfather—coupled to the Zero-Spindle at 500 fathoms can save the Flats and free the jammed counterweight stalling Vesper's air.\nHops 40-43: At the 500-fathom mercury pool, Kael slots the Dry Lease plates. Mara pulls a submerged lever for a 90-second 'grasshopper' spring, extracts the 62-pin gear from Kael, and hammers it past a steel burr on the Zero-Spindle using the lead ledger. With no time to fit the jumper-pinion, she presses Kael's open sternum directly to the spinning Datum gear. The engine restarts, using Kael's heart as the escapement.",
  "instructions_for_next_hop": "Increment 'hop' by 1. Replace 'text' with the next ~250-word installment of the story (keep 'text' around 250 words total, updating 'summary_so_far' and 'story_bible' to retain memory across the 100 hops). Output ONLY valid JSON.",
  "text": "Every second, the Zero-Spindle advanced by one tooth, and every second, the terrible torque of five hundred fathoms of ocean shuddered through Kael’s collarbones. His sea-glass eyes were wide, pinned to the vaulted ceiling where grease-blackened chains hauled the Third Counterweight out of the abyss. A fine spray of whale-oil and blood misted from his sternum with each click.\n\n\"Don't touch the frame,\" Kael rasped, his voice a high, reedy whistle in the twelve-bar helium, clipped into one-second syllables by the kick of the pallet. \"If—my—ribs—slip—the—spindle—strips.\"\n\nMara braced her knees against his hips to steady him, her hands slick with his warmth and the cold mercury beading on the deck. Above his head, the reading-stylus traced the deep, square-cut notches of the first phosphor-bronze plate. With each step of the cam, massive bronze pistons in the outer wall groaned downward, forcing the sea back through the subterranean aquifers.\n\nA sharp whistle blew from a flared brass speaking-tube bolted to the pillar beside them. Once, twice—the signal for the lower manifold. Then came the wet, metallic wheeze of a man breathing through a copper regulator.\n\n*\"Child,\"* Vesper’s voice rattled from the horn, tinny and vast. *\"The compressors are turning. I have twenty atmospheres in my reserve flasks. Half an inch of the First Plate is read.\"* A dull, booming thud echoed down the voice-pipe, followed by the ring of stressed iron. *\"But the Registry hears the chain. They’ve flooded the upper ballast-shafts—and they are lowering the Dredge.\"* "
}

