  "text": "\"What is the Dredge?\" Mara shouted into the flared brass cone, her voice squeaking like a mouse’s in the dense heliox. Behind her, Kael’s spine arched as the sixty-second tooth bit home, a bell-clear chime ringing through his ribs.\n\n*\"A five-ton grapnel of pig-iron and grapnel-flukes,\"* Vesper wheezed back, the copper diaphragm in his helmet clicking with each breath. *\"They drop it down the chain-well when a polder goes rogue. When it hits the Third Counterweight at three hundred fathoms, its flukes will lock into the links and drag the weight back down. Look to your feet, girl. You must uncouple the boy before the shock hits the spindle, or the recoil will shear him in half.\"*\n\nMara looked down at the vibrating catwalk. Half-submerged in a puddle of silver mercury lay the brass jumper-pinion—an articulated bridging gear the size of a dinner plate, mounted on a swinging yoke.\n\n\"If I pull him back, the Datum gear runs wild!\" she cried.\n\n*\"Don't pull him!\"* Vesper barked. *\"Swing the yoke above his collarbone. There is a forty-millisecond dead-beat between the release of his left pallet and the drop of his right. Drop the pinion into the mesh on the exhale of the tick, then yank him clear on the tock!\"*\n\nMara wiped her bloody palms on her wool skirt and gripped the heavy brass yoke. Kael’s salt-white hair was plastered to his forehead; his splinted left hand rose, the brass Vernier calipers trembling as he tapped two fingers against the iron strut to give her the rhythm. \n\n*Tick—rest—tock. Tick—rest—tock.*"
