  "text": "\"Wait,\" Mara squeaked, her voice high and reedy in the twelve-bar mix. \n\nBefore following him onto the rungs, she lunged back to the Zero-Spindle. Now that the scaffolding pawls had engaged the cavern roof, the great bronze collet had released its bite. She wrenched the sixty-two-pin Datum gear free from the spindle’s grease, wiped the black tallow on her sleeve, and pressed the warm brass wheel back into the open socket of Kael’s sternum.\n\nHis mainspring caught with a sharp,hungry *click*. Colour rushed back into his salt-pale cheeks as the escapement resumed its steady, rapid ticking against his ribs. \n\n*\"Go,\"* he breathed, wrapping a strip of oiled linen around the mangled brass stubs on his left hand.\n\nThey scrambled up the northern piling and into the narrow corkscrew of the service spiral. Beneath them, the cavern thundered with the deep, hydraulic roar of the basalt sluices swallowing the sea above, draining the Grey Flats into the earth's crust. But beside them, separated only by a quarter-inch skin of riveted iron, the central shaft was dying.\n\nAt four hundred and twenty fathoms, the spiral stair pinched inward. The iron wall bulged like a bruised fruit where the tangled reef of chain pressed outward. Every few seconds, the five-ton Dredge settled another inch into the iron nest above, sending a shuddering *clong* through the metal under Mara's boots.\n\nWhen they spilled out onto the four-hundred-fathom landing, the air smelled of sheared steel and cold brine. Across the narrow catwalk, the circular hatch to Vesper’s manifold room was weeping needles of high-pressure spray. Mara’s grandfather’s steel clock-arbor, still jammed through the locking dog, was bent into a terrifying crescent."
