  "text": "The fine mist hissing from the hatch gasket stung Mara’s cheek like hot ash. Behind the thick bullseye glass of the viewport, a copper dome bumped weakly against the frame. Vesper was still alive, his ancient face pale and distorted through the double lenses of water and quartz.\n\n*\"The Dredge isn't just pulling the bulkhead down,\"* Kael piped, his helium-thinned voice tight as he pressed his good right palm against the trembling iron wall. *\"It's squeezing the manifold room like a bladder. The water inside is past twenty bar. If that arbor shears, the hatch will blow us into the chain-well.\"* \n\nInside the flooded chamber, Vesper lifted a leaded glove and tapped three times against the glass—*shave and a haircut*—before pointing downward toward the floor of the landing at Mara’s feet. \n\nMara wiped the stinging salt-fog from her eyes and looked down. Half-buried under a crust of flaking red oxide and dead barnacles sat a bronze wheel marked *BILGE BYPASS — TO NAVE SUMP*. \n\n\"If we bleed the water out of his room into the Nave, the pressure drops,\" Mara squeaked, dropping to her knees. She gripped the wheel’s spokes and heaved. It didn't budge. Three centuries of lime scale had welded the valve stem into its bonnet.\n\nAbove them, in the shaft, the five-ton iron wedge slipped another foot through the tangled nest of chain. The steel clock-arbor in the hatch dog gave a high, musical *ping* as a hairline fracture opened across its spine.\n\n*\"Move,\"* Kael rasped. He knelt beside her, wedging the jagged, sheared stubs of his brass caliper-splint between the valve spoke and the packing nut."
}

