  "summary_so_far": "Hops 1-10: As a coppery tide floods the Grey Flats, Mara salvages a bronze diving bell ('ARCHIVE ZERO') containing Kael, a boy with brass calipers splinted to his fingers, a clockwork chest, and a lead ledger. She inserts a 62-pin gear from her grandfather's clock into his sternum to revive him, and they flee as her stilts-house sinks.\nHops 11-28: At Spire Four, they descend the moon pool in a drop-cage. Mines sever the cable at 150 fathoms, and they crash at 390 fathoms onto the Third Counterweight. Equalizing to twelve-bar heliox, they find Vesper trapped in the flooded manifold room at 400 fathoms and jam Mara's clock arbor into the hatch pinhole to hold the seal.\nHops 29-39: In the Nave, they save three phosphor-bronze plates ('THE DRY LEASE') and send a mercury bomb up the pneumatic tube to destroy the upper receiving room. The Translating Loom reveals the Grey Flats are an artificial polder whose 300-year lease expired when the Registry refused a 600-life 'ballast-tithe.' Only the 62-pin Datum gear coupled to the Zero-Spindle at 500 fathoms can save the Flats.\nHops 40-50: At the 500-fathom mercury pool, Mara couples the 62-pin gear from Kael's chest to the Zero-Spindle. When the Registry drops a five-ton Dredge onto the counterweight at 280 fathoms, Mara dumps 40 tons of ballast mercury. The buoyant scaffolding surges up, ratifying the third plate just as the main chain snaps and Mara shears Kael's caliper-splint in the gears to save his heart.\nHops 51-56: The scaffolding locks into the ceiling pawls, opening the basalt sluices to drain the Flats. Two hundred fathoms of severed chain catch the falling Dredge at 410 fathoms. At 400 fathoms, Mara and Kael drain Vesper's chamber into the Nave sump and unbolt his helmet. Vesper recognizes Hollis's clock-arbor and reveals he has been waiting eighty years for the shift change; he warns them the Registry's Dredge is not just a weight, but a diving caisson carrying demolition crews.",
  "instructions_for_next_hop": "Increment 'hop' by 1. Replace 'text' with the next ~250-word installment of the story (keep 'text' around 250 words total, updating 'summary_so_far' and 'story_bible' to retain memory across the 100 hops). Output ONLY valid JSON.",
  "text": "Mara stared at the ancient man, the copper helmet heavy and cold against her hip. *\"You knew my grandfather?\"*\n\n*\"Knew him? I oiled his escapement,\"* Vesper rasped, his voice a high, nasal whine in the dense helium mix. He reached inside his brass breastplate and uncoupled a secondary rubber tube that hissed with escaping trimix and a smell like camphor and pickled tallow. *\"Hollis took the Datum gear up the ladder in sixty-four when the First Alderman tried to forge a dry-seal with pig-lead. Left me on the manifold to hold the trim-valves until the council came to terms. Then the bastards above flooded the lock to drown the audit.\"* \n\nKael leaned against the dripping bulkhead, cradling his bloody left hand against his ticking ribs. *\"The Lease is struck, Bell-Ringer. We coupled the sixty-two-pin to the Zero-Spindle. The sluices at five hundred are drawing down the Flats.\"*\n\nVesper’s milky eyes dropped to the open brass window in Kael’s sternum, listening to the crisp, double-beat click of the grandfather gear. A slow, cracked smile parted his salted beard. Then the iron floor beneath their boots shuddered—not from the water roaring through the sump below, but from a rhythmic, metallic *clank-clank-clank* echoing down the main shaft from ten fathoms below.\n\nVesper’s smile vanished. He tapped a pressure gauge on the wall whose needle was twitching against the peg.\n\n*\"They dropped the five-ton Dredge on us,\"* Mara said. *\"It’s wedged in the chain pile at four-ten.\"*\n\n*\"That’s no dredge, girl,\"* Vesper whispered, fumbling for the spanner at his belt. *\"That’s the Registry’s Knuckle-Bell. It’s an armored caisson. And those are the ratchet-dogs of its airlock turning.\"*"
}

