  "text": "*\"They have to winch themselves down eighty-five fathoms through the chain-tangle to reach the Nave roof,\"* Vesper rasped, his helium-thinned voice tight as he hooked his bronze spanner into the brass hatch’s dog-latch and wrenched it down. *\"The Venturi chute drops straight through the basalt wall to the upper pawl-gallery at four-eighty-five. It’s dry now that you’ve blown the manifold.\"* \n\nThe narrow oval door swung inward, exhaling a draft that smelled of fossilized kelp and tallow. Inside, a polished copper flume curved steeply down into the dark, ribbed every three feet with leather braking-straps.\n\n*\"One-bar suits are coffins at this depth if you crack a joint-seal,\"* Vesper said, swinging his withered, brine-pickled legs over the lip with surprising agility. *\"Get me to the Trim-Tank injector valves before they light their lances.\"* \n\nHe let go, vanishing down the slick copper throat with a dry, whispering hiss. \n\nKael looked at Mara, the sixty-two-pin gear in his sternum ticking bright and fast through his open linen shirt. *\"Hold my belt with your good hand,\"* he piped, sitting on the rim and wrapping his right arm around the first leather strap to slow them. \n\nMara tucked her grandfather’s bent clock-arbor into her waistband, sat behind him, and gripped the stiff waxed canvas at his waist. Kael released the strap. \n\nThey plunged into the dark. The copper walls blurred beneath them, hot with friction against their boots, spiraling downward through the silent rock while, on the other side of the masonry, the heavy, deliberate *clank-hiss* of the Bailiffs’ iron boots echoed down the main shaft."
