  "summary_so_far": "Hops 1-10: As a coppery tide floods the Grey Flats, Mara salvages a bronze diving bell ('ARCHIVE ZERO') containing Kael, a boy with brass calipers splinted to his fingers, a clockwork chest, and a lead ledger. She inserts a 62-pin gear from her grandfather's clock into his sternum to revive him, and they flee as her stilts-house sinks.\nHops 11-28: At Spire Four, they descend the moon pool in a drop-cage. Mines sever the cable at 150 fathoms, and they crash at 390 fathoms onto the Third Counterweight. Equalizing to twelve-bar heliox, they find Vesper trapped in the flooded manifold room at 400 fathoms and jam Mara's clock arbor into the hatch pinhole to hold the seal.\nHops 29-39: In the Nave, they save three phosphor-bronze plates ('THE DRY LEASE') and send a mercury bomb up the pneumatic tube to destroy the upper receiving room. The Translating Loom reveals the Grey Flats are an artificial polder whose 300-year lease expired when the Registry refused a 600-life 'ballast-tithe.' Only the 62-pin Datum gear coupled to the Zero-Spindle at 500 fathoms can save the Flats.\nHops 40-50: At the 500-fathom mercury pool, Mara couples the 62-pin gear from Kael's chest to the Zero-Spindle. When the Registry drops a five-ton Dredge onto the counterweight at 280 fathoms, Mara dumps 40 tons of ballast mercury. The buoyant scaffolding surges up, ratifying the third plate just as the main chain snaps and Mara shears Kael's caliper-splint in the gears to save his heart.\nHops 51-60: The scaffolding locks into the ceiling pawls, opening the basalt sluices. Mara and Kael free Vesper at 400 fathoms. The Dredge opens—it is the Knuckle-Bell, deploying two Registry Bailiffs in one-bar hard-suits with magnesium lances to burn the pawls. At the 485-fathom pawl-gallery, the trio mans a forty-bar brine cannon, knocking the first Bailiff off the roof, though his severed lance stays wedged in the northeast pawl and their copper supply hose begins to blister.",
  "instructions_for_next_hop": "Increment 'hop' by 1. Replace 'text' with the next ~250-word installment of the story (keep 'text' around 250 words total, updating 'summary_so_far' and 'story_bible' to retain memory across the 100 hops). Output ONLY valid JSON.",
  "text": "The blister of green copper split with a sound like tearing canvas. A razor-thin fan of forty-bar brine hissed sideways across the gallery deck, slicing through a cast-iron stanchion as cleanly as wire through tallow.\n\n*\"Down!\"* Vesper squeaked in the high, reedy heliox.\n\nHe hooked his lead-weighted beard over his shoulder and slammed the bronze spanner backward against the gate valve, killing the pressure just as the forward nozzle pressure collapsed into a pathetic dribble.\n\nThirty feet away, the northeast ceiling pawl finally gave up. With a sound like a cannon-shot, the white-hot bronze tooth sheared off. The entire fifty-ton Zero-Spindle scaffolding dropped six inches on its eastern corner, shuddering violently as the remaining three pawls took the asymmetric load. \n\nThe sudden tilt saved them. The second Bailiff, braced at the edge of the slanted roof to hurl his unlit magnesium lance, lost his footing on the greased iron plates. His two-ton suit skidded sideways; the six-foot iron pipe flew wide, striking the gallery railing in a shower of sparks before tumbling down into the mirror-bright mercury pool fifteen fathoms below.\n\nThe Bailiff did not fall. His left pincer clamped onto the gallery's lower tie-rod, arresting his slide with a screech of metal. Behind the thick quartz bullseye of his helmet, a pale, grease-smeared face glared through the violet twilight. Driven by internal steam-servos, the giant hauled his bulbous cast-iron bulk over the broken railing, stepping directly onto the narrow catwalk.\n\nHe had no lance, only two hydraulic pincers capable of shearing three-inch chain. Kael pushed Mara behind him, drawing the shattered brass Vernier calipers from his belt, while his chest-escapement ticked against the giant's heavy, rhythmic clanking."
}

