  "text": "Strand by strand, the braided steel parted like snapped lute strings, each wire whipping back through the grease-slick fog with a sound like a pistol shot. \n\n*\"The oxygen flask!\"* Kael chirped in the twelve-bar gas, his clockwork chest clicking fast behind his linen shirt. With his uninjured right hand and his teeth, he unbuckled the copper cylinder from the dead Bailiff’s hip just as the last core-wire liquefied into a droplet of white-hot iron.\n\n*Twang.*\n\nFreed of tension, the severed cable whipped skyward into the mist. Above them, the Knuckle-Bell’s winch drum spun wild, its sudden loss of load making the five-ton Dredge lurch sideways against the basalt vault.\n\nOn the canted grating, the two-ton hard-suit tipped back toward the broken guardrail. Mara dropped the roaring lance, grabbed the notched brass tip of Hollis’s clock-arbor protruding from the suit's bronze knee, and yanked it free just as Vesper and Kael put their shoulders against the iron cuirass.\n\nTogether they heaved. The corpse-filled suit toppled over the lip of the fifteen-degree tilt, plunging silently through fifteen fathoms of dense heliox. Two seconds later, it struck the mercury lake below—not sinking into the dense liquid metal, but bouncing once with a dull, thunderous *floomp* before bobbing like an iron cork on the silver mirror.\n\nRelieved of the deadweight, the southeast pawl stopped crying. Yet the relief lasted only a heartbeat. Above the fog, the Knuckle-Bell vented its buoyancy tanks with a deafening hiss, dropping straight down to crush the gallery with its five-ton hull."
}

