  "text": "The rusted iron belly of the Knuckle-Bell loomed out of the tallow-fog, five tons of riveted boiler-plate dropping straight onto the cracked southeast pawl. \n\n*\"The cannon!\"* Vesper squeaked in the thin heliox. \n\nThe old bell-ringer threw his driftwood-pale weight onto the bronze gimbal of the fire-suppression monitor. Kael dropped the copper oxygen flask onto the grating and slammed his shoulder beside Vesper’s, forcing the nozzle skyward until its brass stops screeched. Together they wrenched the gate-valve open.\n\nA solid column of twelve-bar brine erupted vertically into the descending hull. It did not stop the five-ton Dredge, but the hydraulic hammer-blow struck the curved starboard ballast-skirt off-center. In the dense gas, the Knuckle-Bell slewed sideways like a struck bell-clapper.\n\nIt missed the southeast pawl by inches.\n\nWith an ear-splitting *crunch* of shearing rivets, the Dredge slammed onto the canted outer ribs of the Zero-Spindle’s roof, ten feet down the fifteen-degree slope. The entire scaffolding shuddered. The three remaining ceiling pawls shrieked in their basalt sockets, showering the catwalk in powdered stone, before holding fast.\n\nThe Knuckle-Bell slid toward the edge, its iron skids shrieking across the phosphor-bronze trusses, and wedged itself against the bent stanchions of the outer guardrail. Inside the iron sphere, muffled shouts and the clatter of dropped wrenches echoed through the hull.\n\nMara picked up the roaring magnesium lance. The white-hot tip hissed as brine spray touched it. *\"Bring the flask,\"* she chirped to Kael, stepping down the steep grating toward the trapped machine."
