  "text": "Mara let the dead brass handle of the lance clatter onto the grating. The smell of burnt tallow and ozone hung thick in the twelve-bar mist. High above, the severed hoist-cable vanished into the dark shaft of Spire Four, its steel tail still thrashing against the wet basalt walls like a dying eel.\n\nInside the Knuckle-Bell, the winchman’s pale face vanished from the quartz bull’s-eye. A moment later, the sphere’s external hydraulic rams hissed. Two heavy iron crab-claws—the Dredge’s salvage manipulators—unfolded from recesses in its lower skirt, their serrated pincers clacking blindly before clamping onto the Zero-Spindle’s upper chord.\n\n*\"He isn't trying to climb,*\" Kael piped, unbuckling the empty copper flask from his hip and letting it roll against the toe-board. The 62-pin gear in his sternum ticked with a rapid, metallic urgency. *\"Look at the belly ring. He’s unpinning the lead keel!\"*\n\nBeneath the Dredge’s riveted hemisphere, a two-ton collar of cast lead hung from a single hydraulic slip-hook. If the winchman dropped his ballast while the claws held the truss, the sudden three-ton surge of positive buoyancy would wrench the outer rib upward and snap the cracked southeast pawl just as surely as the hoist-cable had.\n\nVesper hobbled forward, swinging his heavy bronze spanner against the manipulator’s knuckle-joint, but the two-inch iron tubing barely rang. *\"We can’t break the grip!\"*\n\nMara looked at the empty copper oxygen flask at Kael’s feet, then at the four-inch quartz bull’s-eye port staring out from the Dredge’s curved flank. \n\n*\"Don't hit the claw,*\" Mara squeaked, reaching for Vesper’s spanner. *\"Wedge the slip-hook!\"*"
