  "text": "Mara didn't wait for the old man. She scrambled over the bent stanchion onto the slick curve of the outer rib, pulling Hollis’s bent steel clock-arbor from her belt. Beneath the Knuckle-Bell’s dripping belly, the hydraulic clevis was already retracting, its greased bronze tongue sliding out of the keeper loop that held the two-ton lead collar.\n\nA half-inch gap yawned as the weight shifted. The entire iron sphere groaned, its manipulator claws biting deeper into the Zero-Spindle’s truss.\n\nMara jammed the tapered tip of the clock-arbor straight into the parting clevis pin. The hardened steel spindle—forged a century ago to bear the deadweight of the stilts-house pendulum—caught the edge of the keeper loop. A heartbeat later, Vesper’s heavy bronze spanner slammed down like a maul against the arbor’s butt, driving it three inches deep into the hydraulic housing.\n\nHigh-pressure mineral oil sprayed from a ruptured seal, stinging Mara’s cheeks with the reek of hot whale-fat. The lead keel dropped half an inch, bit down on the wedged arbor with a dull *thud*, and held fast.\n\nInside the quartz bull’s-eye, the winchman’s shadow froze, frantically working a dead lever. \n\nKael climbed onto the rib beside Mara, his bandaged left hand pressed over the rapid ticking in his breastbone. He looked down at the five-ton sphere pinned to their scaffolding, then up into the dark, silent throat of Spire Four.\n\n*\"He’s out of oil,*\" Kael piped, his helium-thinned voice echoing strangely against the wet iron hull. *\"And we’re out of air-time. When the sluices finish draining the Flats, the moon pool seal will drop back to one bar.\"*"
