  "summary_so_far": "Hops 1-10: As a coppery tide floods the Grey Flats, Mara salvages a bronze diving bell ('ARCHIVE ZERO') containing Kael, a boy with brass calipers splinted to his fingers, a clockwork chest, and a lead ledger. She inserts a 62-pin gear from her grandfather's clock into his sternum to revive him, and they flee as her stilts-house sinks.\nHops 11-28: At Spire Four, they descend the moon pool in a drop-cage. Mines sever the cable at 150 fathoms, and they crash at 390 fathoms onto the Third Counterweight. Equalizing to twelve-bar heliox, they find Vesper trapped in the flooded manifold room at 400 fathoms and jam Mara's clock arbor into the hatch pinhole to hold the seal.\nHops 29-39: In the Nave, they save three phosphor-bronze plates ('THE DRY LEASE') and send a mercury bomb up the pneumatic tube to destroy the upper receiving room. The Translating Loom reveals the Grey Flats are an artificial polder whose 300-year lease expired when the Registry refused a 600-life 'ballast-tithe.' Only the 62-pin Datum gear coupled to the Zero-Spindle at 500 fathoms can save the Flats.\nHops 40-50: At the 500-fathom mercury pool, Mara couples the 62-pin gear from Kael's chest to the Zero-Spindle. When the Registry drops a five-ton Dredge onto the counterweight at 280 fathoms, Mara dumps 40 tons of ballast mercury. The buoyant scaffolding surges up, ratifying the third plate just as the main chain snaps and Mara shears Kael's caliper-splint in the gears to save his heart.\nHops 51-69: The scaffolding locks into the ceiling pawls, opening the basalt sluices. Mara and Kael free Vesper at 400 fathoms. At the 485-fathom pawl-gallery, they kill two Registry Bailiffs, deflect the falling Knuckle-Bell onto the roof ribs, sever its hoist cable, and jam Hollis's clock-arbor into its keel clevis before the winchman can drop the two-ton lead ballast to tear the roof down. Kael warns that when the Flats finish draining, the moon pool seal will fail and decompress the Nave.",
  "instructions_for_next_hop": "Increment 'hop' by 1. Replace 'text' with the next ~250-word installment of the story (keep 'text' around 250 words total, updating 'summary_so_far' and 'story_bible' to retain memory across the 100 hops). Output ONLY valid JSON.",
  "text": "Below them, five hundred fathoms down, the basalt sluices thundered like a subterranean cataract. Every ton of brackish water swallowed by the trench lowered the hydrostatic head pressing against the moon pool at four hundred fathoms. When the column thinned, the twelve-bar heliox bubble keeping the Nave dry would erupt upward through the shaft, boiling the nitrogen and helium straight out of their blood.\n\n*\"A twelve-bar blowdown takes forty hours on the staging tables,\"* Vesper squeaked, his milky eyes fixed on the weeping iron hull of the Knuckle-Bell. He tapped the sphere’s dorsal skirt with his bronze spanner. *\"We don’t have forty minutes. But the Registry built their Dredges to hold thirty atmospheres from the inside or the out.\"* \n\nMara wiped the stinging mineral oil from her cheek and looked through the thick quartz bull’s-eye. The winchman—a pinch-faced clerk in a greased wool waistcoat—was staring back at them, his hands trembling on the dead hydraulic manifold. Inside that five-ton iron walnut, the air was still surface-normal, one bar of stale sea-breeze.\n\n*\"If we crack his equalizing valve from the outside,\"* Mara said, her tinny voice sharp in the fog, *\"the twelve-bar air rushes in. It’ll crush his eardrums, maybe worse, if he doesn't clear them. Then we unbolt the ventral hatch, climb in, seal it behind us, and pull Hollis's arbor.\"* \n\n*\"Drop the lead keel after the claws are bled,*\" Kael murmured, his chest-gears clicking briskly against his ribs. *\"The bell shoots up the flooded shaft like a cork, holding our twelve atmospheres inside while the world decompresses around us.\"* \n\nVesper fitted the jaws of his spanner over the brass cover of the Dredge's external bleed-cock."
}

