  "text": "The freezing condensation fog blinded her, thick as tallow smoke and tasting of pulverized rust. Above the roar of the erupting moon pool, the three bronze grapple-claws of the Knuckle-Bell groaned as the sphere’s positive buoyancy fought against the dead weight of its five-ton cast-iron shoe.\n\nMara braced her shoulder against the greasy curve of the iron keel, felt through the mist for her grandfather’s bent steel clock-arbor, and swung the heavy bronze spanner with both hands.\n\n*Clang.*\n\nSparks flared dull orange in the dense heliox. The arbor didn't budge. Below the scaffolding, a second mammoth bubble tore loose from the four-hundred-fathom lip of the shaft; the pressure in the Nave plummeted another full bar, and a sharp pain stabbed behind Mara’s eyes as the gas in her sinuses expanded. \n\nShe choked, swung the spanner again, and caught the tapered tip of Hollis’s spindle square. \n\nThe steel pin shot free—Mara snatched it out of the air with her left hand—and the slip-hook parted with the report of a cannon. Five tons of cast iron dropped away into the white void, smashing through the lower catwalks in a cascade of snapping tie-rods before plunging into the dark water below.\n\nFreed of its keel-weight, the hollow bronze sphere surged upward six inches, slamming its locked grapple-claws against the underside of the roof ribs with a violence that nearly knocked Mara off the beam. \n\nKael’s salt-stained hand reached down through the ventral coaming, his linen-wrapped caliper-splint hooked like a gaff. *\"Up! Grab my wrist!\"*\n\nMara tossed the spanner and the clock-arbor inside, caught his forearm, and scrambled through the hatchway just as Vesper threw his frail weight against the heavy iron plug to swing it shut."
