  "text": "Gravity doubled, crushing Mara against the floor grating alongside the unconscious Winchman’s ribs. Shards of lantern glass bit into her palms, slick with warm castor oil and whale tallow. The bronze hull rang like a struck anvil as it ricocheted off the riveted iron guide-rails of the shaft, spinning slowly on its vertical axis in the roaring updraft of expanding gas and froth.\n\n*\"Three hundred fathoms!\"* Vesper’s helium-thinned voice warbled out of the dark. Even blind, the old keeper was reading the ascent through his fingertips pressed to the weeping bronze sweat of the hull. *\"The column’s turning to foam! We’re outrunning the water!\"*\n\nBeside Mara’s ear, the sixty-two-pin Datum gear in Kael’s chest ticked with crisp, metallic urgency, each beat accompanied by a tiny blue spark from his dynamo coils that briefly illuminated the cramped sphere: Vesper’s lead-braided beard floating upward in the dense air, the three grooved plates of the Dry Lease wedged under Kael’s boots, and the brass pressure-gauge needle straining against its twelve-bar pin as the exterior sea-pressure plummeted.\n\n*\"One-eighty!\"* Vespershrilled as the hull scraped along a flange with a sound like a giant file tearing sheet-iron. *\"Mind the snare!\"*\n\nMara’s stomach dropped. At one hundred and fifty fathoms hung the severed three-inch steel hawser of their old drop-cage—and the ring of unexploded contact mines the Registry had moored in the throat. \n\nSomething heavy whipped across the top dome with a wet, metallic *whack*. A braided steel strand screeched across the quartz bullseye, throwing a ribbon of orange friction-sparks through the frothing water outside, and the Knuckle-Bell snagged, tipping forty degrees on its side."
}

