  "text": "The bronze shears bit into the grease-blackened steel. Wire by wire, the three-inch hawser parted with sharp, harp-string twangs that vibrated through the floorboards and up into Mara’s teeth. Outside the quartz viewport, the rusted contact mine drifted closer, drawn into the low-pressure wake behind the bell’s shoulder until one of its lead-sheathed glass horns scraped a pale streak across the exterior bronze.\n\n*\"Hold the Lease plates flat!\"* Kael squeaked through the dense helium. He threw his weight onto the hydraulic release lever.\n\nThe shears snapped shut. The final core-strand parted with a dull *thwack*, and the unballasted Knuckle-Bell shot upward like a cork released in a bottle. \n\nGravity crushed them to the oily deck. Below them, the recoiling remnant of the hawser whipped backward, slapping squarely across the mine’s cluster of acid-horns. Three fathoms beneath their rising keel, the dark water turned blinding, magnesium-white. \n\nA sledgehammer of incompressible water struck the underside of the bell. The bronze hull rang like a cathedral gong, so loud that fresh blood welled from the Winchman’s ruptured ears and Vesper clapped his scarred palms over his head. Sympathetic detonations rippled across the necklace of mines at the hundred-and-fifty-fathom collar—*boom, boom, boom*—each shockwave kicking the rising sphere harder into the boiling updraft.\n\nMara lay pinned against the phosphor-bronze tablets, watching the depth gauge needle spin backward past eighty fathoms, then sixty, then forty. As the external water pressure vanished, the twelve atmospheres of trapped heliox inside began to push outward, making the inward-sealing hatch dogs groan in their sockets."
